🚀 DAY 19 — CONDITIONAL RENDERING

Today we're going to learn one of the most important concepts in React:

How to show different UI depending on a condition.

Think about websites you use every day:

User logged in?
      ↓
   YES ─────→ Show Dashboard
      ↓
    NO ─────→ Show Login

That's conditional rendering.

1. What is Conditional Rendering?
Simple definition

Conditional rendering means displaying different UI depending on a condition.

For example:

function App() {
  const isLoggedIn = true;


  return (
    <div>
      {isLoggedIn ? <h1>Welcome</h1> : <h1>Please Login</h1>}
    </div>
  );
}

If:

isLoggedIn = true

React displays:

Welcome

If:

isLoggedIn = false

React displays:

Please Login
2. Why Do We Need It?

Imagine you're building an e-commerce website.

When the user is logged in:

┌──────────────────────────┐
│ Welcome, Ithesh          │
│                          │
│ My Orders                │
│ My Profile               │
│ Logout                   │
└──────────────────────────┘

When the user is not logged in:

┌──────────────────────────┐
│ Welcome                  │
│                          │
│ Please Login             │
│                          │
│ [ Login ]                │
└──────────────────────────┘

You don't want to create two completely different applications.

Instead, you use a condition.

                User
                 │
                 ▼
          Is user logged in?
             /       \
           YES       NO
            │         │
            ▼         ▼
       Dashboard     Login

This is why conditional rendering is extremely important.

3. Real-World Analogy

Imagine a traffic signal 🚦.

The signal checks the current condition:

Light = RED
   ↓
STOP


Light = GREEN
   ↓
GO


Light = YELLOW
   ↓
SLOW DOWN

The same idea happens in React.

Condition
   ↓
React decides
   ↓
Which UI should appear?

For example:

isLoggedIn = true
       ↓
Show Dashboard

or:

isLoggedIn = false
       ↓
Show Login
4. Syntax

There are several ways to perform conditional rendering in React.

We'll learn the most important ones.

Method 1 — if
if (condition) {
  // code
}
Method 2 — Ternary operator
condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse

Example:

isLoggedIn ? <Dashboard /> : <Login />
Method 3 — Logical AND &&
condition && <Component />

Example:

isLoggedIn && <LogoutButton />
Method 4 — Logical OR ||
value || fallback

We'll learn when this is useful and also discuss its common problems.

5. Line-by-Line Explanation

Let's start with the simplest example.

function App() {
  const isLoggedIn = true;


  return (
    <div>
      {isLoggedIn ? <h1>Welcome</h1> : <h1>Please Login</h1>}
    </div>
  );
}

Let's understand every part.

Line 1
function App() {

function

Means we're creating a JavaScript function.

App

This is the name of our React component.

()

The component currently doesn't receive props.

{

Starts the function body.

Line 2
const isLoggedIn = true;

const

Creates a JavaScript variable whose binding cannot be reassigned.

isLoggedIn

Our variable name.

=

Assigns a value.

true

A Boolean value.

Boolean means:

true
false

So:

isLoggedIn = true

means:

The user is logged in.

Line 4
return (

return

Tells the function what it should return.

Our component returns JSX.

Line 5
<div>

Creates a div element.

Line 6
{isLoggedIn ? <h1>Welcome</h1> : <h1>Please Login</h1>}

This is the important line.

The structure is:

condition
   ?
   value if TRUE
   :
   value if FALSE

So:

isLoggedIn
    ?
<h1>Welcome</h1>
    :
<h1>Please Login</h1>

Because:

isLoggedIn = true

React chooses:

<h1>Welcome</h1>
{ }

In JSX:

{}

allows us to insert JavaScript expressions.

For example:

<h1>{name}</h1>

or:

<h1>{isLoggedIn ? "Welcome" : "Login"}</h1>
?

The ? belongs to the ternary operator.

It means:

If the condition is true, use this.

:

Means:

Otherwise, use this.

