9. Conditional Rendering with if

Ternary isn't always the best choice.

Sometimes the condition is more complicated.

For example:

function App() {
  const isLoggedIn = true;


  if (isLoggedIn) {
    return <h1>Dashboard</h1>;
  }


  return <h1>Login Page</h1>;
}


export default App;

Flow:

App()
 │
 ▼
isLoggedIn?
 │
 ├── true ──→ Dashboard
 │
 └── false ─→ Login Page

This is often easier to read when the UI is large.

10. Ternary vs if
Ternary

Good for short conditions:

return isLoggedIn ? <Dashboard /> : <Login />;
if

Good for more complex logic:

if (isLoading) {
  return <Loading />;
}


if (error) {
  return <ErrorMessage />;
}


return <Dashboard />;

This pattern is extremely common in real React applications.

11. Logical AND &&

Now let's learn another very important technique.

Suppose you only want to show a logout button when the user is logged in.

You don't need an else.

You can write:

function App() {
  const isLoggedIn = true;


  return (
    <div>
      <h1>My Website</h1>


      {isLoggedIn && <button>Logout</button>}
    </div>
  );
}

The logic is:

isLoggedIn
    │
    ▼
 true?
  │
  ▼
Show Logout

If:

isLoggedIn = false;

the button isn't rendered.

12. Understanding &&

This:

isLoggedIn && <button>Logout</button>

means approximately:

If isLoggedIn is true
        ↓
show button

Example:

true && "Hello"

produces:

Hello

But:

false && "Hello"

produces:

false

React doesn't render false as visible text.

Therefore:

false && <button>Logout</button>

results in no button.

13. Multiple Conditions

Suppose an application has three states:

Loading
Error
Success

You could write:

function App() {
  const isLoading = false;
  const hasError = false;


  if (isLoading) {
    return <h1>Loading...</h1>;
  }


  if (hasError) {
    return <h1>Something went wrong</h1>;
  }


  return <h1>Data loaded successfully</h1>;
}

Flow:

             App
              │
              ▼
          Is Loading?
          /        \
        YES         NO
         │           │
         ▼           ▼
      Loading     Has Error?
                    /    \
                  YES     NO
                   │       │
                   ▼       ▼
                 Error   Success

This pattern is extremely important when working with APIs.

14. Common Mistakes
❌ Mistake 1 — Using if directly inside JSX

This is invalid:

return (
  <div>
    if (isLoggedIn) {
      <h1>Welcome</h1>
    }
  </div>
);

You cannot use a normal if statement directly inside JSX like this.

Instead use:

{isLoggedIn && <h1>Welcome</h1>}

or:

{isLoggedIn ? <h1>Welcome</h1> : <h1>Login</h1>}
❌ Mistake 2 — Forgetting the {}

Wrong:

<div>
  isLoggedIn ? <h1>Welcome</h1> : <h1>Login</h1>
</div>

Correct:

<div>
  {isLoggedIn ? <h1>Welcome</h1> : <h1>Login</h1>}
</div>

Why?

Because {} tells JSX:

Evaluate this as JavaScript.

❌ Mistake 3 — Confusing = and ===

Wrong:

if (isLoggedIn = true)

This assigns a value.

Usually you want:

if (isLoggedIn === true)

Or simply:

if (isLoggedIn)
15. Interview Questions
Q1. What is conditional rendering?

Answer:

Conditional rendering is the technique of displaying different UI based on a condition.

Q2. How can you perform conditional rendering in React?

Common approaches include:

if / else
ternary operator
logical &&
logical ||
Q3. What is the ternary operator?

It is a JavaScript operator with three parts:

condition ? trueValue : falseValue
Q4. Can we use if directly inside JSX?

No.

Normal if statements cannot be placed directly inside JSX expressions.

Instead, use:

if

before the return, or use:

ternary

or:

&&

inside JSX.

Q5. What is the difference between && and ternary?

&& is useful when you only want to render something when a condition is true.

isLoggedIn && <Logout />

Ternary is useful when you have both true and false UI:

isLoggedIn ? <Dashboard /> : <Login />
